    }
  ],
  "cause_assessment": "The snapshots establish different underlying causes. Source A indicates a deployment or image registry failure where the new image manifest is unavailable, preventing the new Pod from starting. Source B indicates a runtime failure where the existing image is running but failing readiness checks due to a dependency timeout. These are distinct failure modes: one is a provisioning/infrastructure issue, and the other is an application/dependency issue. They cannot be established as the same root cause.",
  "alternative_explanation": "A plausible alternative is that a single upstream change (e.g., a bad image push or a dependency outage) triggered both symptoms simultaneously, but the provided evidence does not link them causally. The lack of logged errors in one source does not rule out a fault in the other.",
  "recovery": "not_established",
  "recovery_reason": "Recovery evidence is missing. Source A shows a new Pod failing to pull, but it does not confirm that a specific healthy previous revision is available or that rolling back would resolve the dependency timeout seen in Source B. Without proof that a previous revision is healthy and addresses both symptoms, rollback is not justified.",
  "next_read_only_checks": [
    "Check the registry for the existence and validity of the image manifest referenced in Source A.",
    "Inspect the dependency service logs to determine if the timeout in Source B is due to a specific downstream failure.",
    "Verify the status of the previous revision to confirm it is healthy and available for rollback."
  ]
}

